Ahead of starting development of the policy linkage solution, I wanted to share a few data coverage considerations from our review of GPPS policy data across the 54 in-scope markets, and seek your alignment on how we proceed.

What we found

44 markets have country-level policy data. 10 (Argentina, Chile, Guernsey, Israel, Isle of Man, Jersey, Cayman Islands, Saudi Arabia, Uruguay and Canada) have none in their country tables. Global policies are visible for some of these markets.
For some policies, the substantive text sits only in an attached PDF rather than in the policy record or table. In Brazil, for example, neither of its two policies has any textual data in the table; all of it is contained in the attached PDF. France is another market with a sizeable number of such attachments.
Linked RSM details appear to be missing for most countries.

Why it matters

Markets with no country-level data cannot be linked to local policies, so coverage there will rely on global policies where available.
Policies whose content sits only in attachments have no usable text in the table for matching, so they cannot be reliably linked until that content is available.
Without RSM details, our ability to validate links in those countries is limited.

Way forward

Proceed with development on the data currently available, with these coverage limitations documented.
Treat the 10 markets and attachment-only policies as limited coverage in the initial phase, to be revisited if the data becomes available.
Track these as open dependencies with the relevant data owners.

If you would prefer these gaps to be resolved before development starts, please let me know. I’ll set up a call on Monday to walk through the details, and a country-wise breakdown is attached for reference.
